WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.500
If the brain trusts the stretch line, what turns stretch into a signal on it?

2
00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:12.300
A candidate is Piezo2, a mechanically activated channel.

3
00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:14.400
Force opens it.

4
00:00:15.033 --> 00:00:18.073
Woo and colleagues tested it in mice, in three ways.

5
00:00:19.033 --> 00:00:20.413
First, where it is.

6
00:00:21.133 --> 00:00:25.913
A reporter showed Piezo2 in the sensory endings on spindles and tendon organs.

7
00:00:27.033 --> 00:00:28.713
Second, what current it carries.

8
00:00:29.593 --> 00:00:33.433
Poked cell bodies of labelled neurons gave rapidly adapting currents.

9
00:00:34.483 --> 00:00:36.323
Third, does the ending need it?

10
00:00:37.003 --> 00:00:41.663
They used Cre driver lines to remove Piezo two in the cells those lines reach,

11
00:00:42.103 --> 00:00:43.603
including proprioceptors.

12
00:00:44.463 --> 00:00:47.063
Neither driver is specific to proprioceptors.

13
00:00:47.967 --> 00:00:52.207
Then they stretched an isolated muscle and recorded from its sensory nerve.

14
00:00:53.167 --> 00:00:58.867
Predict. With Piezo2 gone from these neurons, what happens to stretch-evoked firing?

15
00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:07.580
It was sharply reduced, with either Cre line.

16
00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:11.160
And the mice moved with severely uncoordinated limbs.

17
00:01:14.233 --> 00:01:15.633
Not quite all of it went.

18
00:01:16.473 --> 00:01:19.613
In one of eight muscles per line, some stretch response remained.

19
00:01:20.667 --> 00:01:24.347
That could be Piezo2 left behind by incomplete deletion.

20
00:01:25.207 --> 00:01:27.087
So it is called the principal transducer.

21
00:01:28.050 --> 00:01:30.690
The leftover does not prove a second channel.

22
00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:32.670
Nor does it rule one out.

23
00:01:33.583 --> 00:01:37.083
So Piezo2 is the principal channel on the stretch line.

24
00:01:37.963 --> 00:01:39.983
It starts the signal the brain reads as stretch.

25
00:01:41.017 --> 00:01:47.677
Now a puzzle. In cultured cell bodies, the current fades with a time constant of 5.6

26
00:01:47.677 --> 00:01:48.677
milliseconds.

27
00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:50.700
Assume one exponential.

28
00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:54.760
By 20 milliseconds, only about 3% % is left.

29
00:01:55.783 --> 00:01:57.263
Zoom out to one second.

30
00:01:58.103 --> 00:01:59.563
The curve lies flat on the floor.

31
00:02:00.616 --> 00:02:04.596
Yet a spindle must report a muscle held at a new length for seconds.

32
00:02:04.596 --> 00:02:08.096
And Piezo2 is needed for that held response.

33
00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:11.080
So how does a channel this fast do it?

34
00:02:11.983 --> 00:02:15.523
A cell body in a dish may not behave like an ending in muscle.

35
00:02:16.503 --> 00:02:18.883
Other channels may carry part of the held response.

36
00:02:19.933 --> 00:02:22.253
And the ending has machinery of its own.

37
00:02:23.213 --> 00:02:26.633
Vesicle-released glutamate helps sustain firing in a held stretch.

38
00:02:27.513 --> 00:02:29.053
The full pathway is not settled.

39
00:02:30.083 --> 00:02:33.683
Piezo2 is the principal transducer on the stretch line.

40
00:02:34.583 --> 00:02:36.663
How it supports a held signal is still open.
